---
import LabsLayout from "../../layouts/labs.astro";
---

<LabsLayout title="Packing script — Querial Labs" active="packing">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-indigo-on-dark">Labs</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">Packing script</h1>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-3 max-w-2xl">
                <code class="text-foreground">build-packages.ps1</code> copies canonical
                SQL from <code class="text-foreground">migrations/</code> and
                <code class="text-foreground">steps/</code> into valid
                <code class="text-foreground">querial.package/v1</code> trees. You do not
                hand-edit generated YAML for day-to-day lab work — edit the SQL, then re-run the script.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
            <a href="/labs/adventureworks/downloads/adventureworks-source.zip" download
               class="inline-flex items-center rounded-lg bg-querial-indigo px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-white hover:opacity-90">
                Download lab source
            </a>
            <a href="/labs/adventureworks/source/build-packages.ps1" download
               class="inline-flex items-center rounded-lg border border-border/60 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium hover:bg-muted/50">
                Download build-packages.ps1
            </a>
            <a href="/labs/adventureworks/source/verify-connections.ps1" download
               class="inline-flex items-center rounded-lg border border-border/60 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium hover:bg-muted/50">
                Download verify-connections.ps1
            </a>
        </div>

        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground">
            <h2>Layout after unzip</h2>
            <pre class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>adventureworks-source/
  build-packages.ps1
  verify-connections.ps1
  README.md
  fixtures/
    tiny-ids.parquet          # Scenario F trigger file (parquet.ids)
  migrations/
    sqlserver/     001_create_schema.sql … 006_create_sales_summary.sql
    postgres/      same codes, plus 007/008 ingest schema for F
  steps/
    A-sqlserver-only/
    B-postgres-only/
    C-both/
    D-fanout/
    E-artifacts/
    F-ingest/</code></pre>
            <p>
                Keep this folder together. The script copies SQL using paths relative to
                itself. Do not run it from a folder that only contains the <code class="text-foreground">.ps1</code>.
            </p>

            <h2>What it emits</h2>
            <pre class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>packages/
  scenario-a-sqlserver/          # tree
  scenario-a-sqlserver.zip       # with -Zip
  scenario-b-postgres/
  scenario-c-both/
  scenario-d-fanout/
  scenario-e-artifacts/
  scenario-f-ingest/
  aw-lab-all/                    # with -Combined
  aw-lab-all.zip</code></pre>
            <p>
                Each tree contains <code class="text-foreground">querial.package.yaml</code>,
                <code class="text-foreground">connections/catalog.yaml</code> (stubs only:
                name, provider, description), <code class="text-foreground">pipelines/&lt;code&gt;/pipeline.yaml</code>,
                copied SQL, and a per-pipeline README. Re-running overwrites those folders.
                The script never writes connection strings or passwords.
            </p>

            <h2>Parameters</h2>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
                <li><code class="text-foreground">-Scenarios A,B,C,D,E,F</code> — subset to build. Default is all six. Comma-separated or multiple args. Unknown letters throw.</li>
                <li><code class="text-foreground">-Zip</code> — write a zip next to each tree. Prefer this for Workspace import (the modal expects a zip, 64&nbsp;MB max).</li>
                <li><code class="text-foreground">-Validate</code> — after each tree, run the Package CLI if <code class="text-foreground">QUERIAL_PACKAGE_CLI</code> points at a <code class="text-foreground">.csproj</code>; otherwise a structural check (manifest, catalog names, referenced SQL files exist).</li>
                <li><code class="text-foreground">-Combined</code> — also emit <code class="text-foreground">aw-lab-all</code> with every selected pipeline and a union catalog. Connection name clashes with different providers fail closed.</li>
                <li><code class="text-foreground">-OutDir</code> — override the packages folder (relative to the script, or absolute).</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Commands</h2>
            <pre class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>cd path\to\adventureworks-source

# All six trees
.\build-packages.ps1

# Trees + zips + structural (or Package CLI) validation
.\build-packages.ps1 -Zip -Validate

# Only C, then only D and E
.\build-packages.ps1 -Scenarios A,C
.\build-packages.ps1 -Scenarios D,E -Validate

# Per-scenario packages plus aw-lab-all
.\build-packages.ps1 -Combined -Zip -Validate
.\build-packages.ps1 -Combined -Scenarios A,B -OutDir .\packages</code></pre>

            <h2>Zips: Package CLI vs Compress-Archive</h2>
            <p>
                If you set <code class="text-foreground">$env:QUERIAL_PACKAGE_CLI</code> to
                the Package CLI project file, <code class="text-foreground">-Zip</code> calls
                <code class="text-foreground">dotnet run --project … -- pack &lt;tree&gt; &lt;zip&gt;</code>
                and <code class="text-foreground">-Validate</code> calls
                <code class="text-foreground">validate</code>. That path is checksum-stable
                and matches what Querial’s import validator expects. Format, tree, and
                import rules:
                <a href="/docs/developer-packages" class="text-link hover:underline">Packages</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Without that environment variable, <code class="text-foreground">-Zip</code>
                uses PowerShell <code class="text-foreground">Compress-Archive</code> on the
                tree contents. That zip still imports when the layout is valid. Use it for
                local labs; use the CLI when you need bit-stable archives.
            </p>
            <pre class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>$env:QUERIAL_PACKAGE_CLI = "C:\path\to\Querial.PackageCli.csproj"
.\build-packages.ps1 -Zip -Validate</code></pre>

            <h2>How a scenario is defined</h2>
            <p>
                Scenario tables live inside the script (not separate YAML). Each scenario
                has a package code, revision, connection stubs, and one pipeline with:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>Migrations and <code class="text-foreground">migration_dependencies</code> (no <code class="text-foreground">requirement</code> flag on migration edges).</li>
                <li>Steps with type, connection, SQL path, optional <code class="text-foreground">config</code> and <code class="text-foreground">failure_policy</code>.</li>
                <li>Step edges: <code class="text-foreground">from</code> → <code class="text-foreground">to</code>, with <code class="text-foreground">requirement</code> of <code class="text-foreground">required</code>, <code class="text-foreground">succeeded</code>, or <code class="text-foreground">artifact_available</code>.</li>
                <li><code class="text-foreground">SqlCopies</code> mapping canonical files into the package tree.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                After you change a <code class="text-foreground">.sql</code> file, re-run
                the script. Import again in Workspace with <strong class="text-foreground">Overwrite existing non-empty drafts</strong>
                if the pipeline code already exists.
            </p>

            <h2>Artifact SQL in the packer (Scenario E)</h2>
            <p>
                Scenario E is the DuckDB teaching package. The script emits three
                <code class="text-foreground">artifact-sql</code> steps (no
                <code class="text-foreground">connection</code>):
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>Single-input CAST/filter: <code class="text-foreground">transform_sales_order</code>, <code class="text-foreground">transform_sales_line</code>.</li>
                <li>Two-input JOIN + GROUP BY: <code class="text-foreground">transform_order_summary</code> with a list of two <code class="text-foreground">from_step</code> rows (the CAST transforms). SQL uses <code class="text-foreground">{"{{ input.sales_order_transformed }}"}</code> and <code class="text-foreground">{"{{ input.sales_line_transformed }}"}</code> — producer output names, not YAML map keys.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Canonical SQL lives in <code class="text-foreground">steps/E-artifacts/</code>
                (including <code class="text-foreground">045_transform_order_summary.sql</code>)
                and E-only migrations <code class="text-foreground">006_create_sales_summary.sql</code>.
                Edit those files, then re-run the script — do not hand-edit generated YAML.
                Map-shaped <code class="text-foreground">inputs:</code> /
                <code class="text-foreground">stages:</code> fail validation.
            </p>

            <h2>External Parquet in the packer (Scenario F)</h2>
            <p>
                Scenario F is the ingest teaching package. The script copies
                <code class="text-foreground">fixtures/tiny-ids.parquet</code> into the
                tree, emits an <code class="text-foreground">external-parquet</code> root
                (<code class="text-foreground">ingest_ids</code>, no connection, no SQL),
                and a staged PostgreSQL load. Canonical SQL lives in
                <code class="text-foreground">steps/F-ingest/</code> and migrations
                <code class="text-foreground">007_create_ingest_schema.sql</code> /
                <code class="text-foreground">008_create_ingest_ids.sql</code>.
                Do not schedule the published version.
                <a href="/labs/scenario-f" class="text-link hover:underline">Scenario F</a>,
                <a href="/docs/developer-handler-contracts" class="text-link hover:underline">handler contracts</a>.
            </p>

            <h2>verify-connections.ps1</h2>
            <p>
                Optional pre-flight. It does <strong class="text-foreground">not</strong>
                apply Querial migrations. It checks AdventureWorks
                <code class="text-foreground">Person.Person</code>, that the SQL Server
                destination catalog opens, optional <code class="text-foreground">psql</code>
                inside a Docker container named <code class="text-foreground">target-pg</code>
                (override with <code class="text-foreground">-PgContainerName</code>), and
                TCP to <code class="text-foreground">-PgHost:-PgPort</code>.
            </p>
            <pre class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>.\verify-connections.ps1
.\verify-connections.ps1 -AdventureWorksDb AdventureWorks2022 -QuerialSqlDb YourDestName
.\verify-connections.ps1 -PgHost 127.0.0.1 -PgPort 15434 -PgPassword '&lt;local&gt;'</code></pre>
            <p>
                Pass <code class="text-foreground">-PgPassword</code> only when you want
                the in-container query. Do not commit real passwords. Fix failures before import.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</LabsLayout>
